with heightened restrictions and strict  capacity limits many restaurants are  having to get really creative in order  to make ends meet 8 news now reporter  kate houston live at tres casuelas on  spring mountain road in polaris  with the changes they're making kate to  stay open  yes this year restaurants have had to  adapt time and time again in order to  stay  open here at tres casuelas you can see  they built an  outdoor dining area over some parking  spaces this is to increase their  capacity  limit and to offer more options to  guests now the owner angelo reyes says  his business was impacted drastically by  the most recent restrictions  he says all of the costs to keep the  restaurant open including  overhead and labor is far more expensive  than what 25 percent capacity brings in  reyes and his team had to get creative  with the limitations that they had  in just five days they were able to  build this outdoor patio  which reyes calls a new era of dining he  says while it was a bit of a rush  decision  it's been a game changer a push for us  to be more aggressive  and invest and really gamble against  this economy that we're facing  by making an investment like building  this patio otherwise  my business at 25 percent will never be  able to survive  this isn't the first time reyes has had  to make changes in march he developed a  price conscious menu with restaurant  staples  as well as essential grocery items for  those that needed help  back out here live reyes says he always  wanted an outdoor dining area  but he never expected to do it out of  necessity he says that he is  very thankful for all of his customers  that have supported him in the  restaurant  as they make these changes reporting  live from chris casuelas kate houston 8  news now
 

